  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "The provided CSV files contain differential gene expression (DGE) results but lack pathway annotations or GSEA statistics, and the available tools do not support GSEA execution. Here's a breakdown of the input and limitations:\n\n---\n\n### **Introduction/Context**\n- **Mutation/Treatment**: Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy (ICI)-induced myositis (irMyositis) vs. spontaneous dermatomyositis (DM) subtypes (anti-Mi2, anti-TIF1-γ) and non-diseased controls (NDC).  \n- **Tissue/Cell Line**: Skeletal muscle biopsies.  \n- **Assay**: NanoString nCounter PanCancer Immune Profiling Panel for DGE.  \n- **Key Signals**:  \n  - irMyositis vs. DM (Mi2/TIF1y): Downregulation of interferon-stimulated genes (ISGs; e.g., MX1, ISG15, IFIT1) and complement factors (CFB).  \n  - DM vs. NDC: Mixed up/down regulation of inflammatory mediators (e.g., S100A7, CCL22).  \n  - irMyositis vs. NDC: Upregulation of MAPK1 and HLA genes, downregulation of IFNB1 and PBK.\n\n---\n\n### **Keywords**\n- Immune checkpoint inhibitors, myositis, dermatomyositis (DM), interferon signaling, complement system, MAPK pathway, HLA, cytokines.\n\n---\n\n### **Candidate Genes**\n- **ISGs**: MX1, ISG15, IFIT1, OAS3.  \n- **Inflammatory mediators**: IFNG, CCL22, S100A7.  \n- **Complement pathway**: CFB.  \n- **MAPK signaling**: MAPK1, PBK.  \n- **HLA**: HLA-A, HLA-C.\n\n---\n\n### **Pathway Enrichments**  \n**No GSEA results available** in the provided CSV files. The data includes gene-level DE statistics (logFC, p-values) but lacks pathway annotations (e.g., Reactome terms).  \n\n---\n\n### **Targeted Subset**  \n- **Interferon signaling**: MX1, ISG15, IFIT1, and IFNB1 are downregulated in irMyositis vs. DM, suggesting suppressed type I IFN responses.  \n- **MAPK pathway**: MAPK1 is upregulated in irMyositis vs. NDC, indicating potential pro-inflammatory activation.  \n- **HLA class I**: HLA-A and HLA-C are upregulated in irMyositis vs. NDC, consistent with antigen-presenting cell activity.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| **Gene**   | **Direction** | **Role/Function**               | **Pathway(s)**         |  \n|------------|---------------|----------------------------------|------------------------|  \n| **MX1**    | Down          | Antiviral IFN response           | Type I IFN signaling   |  \n| **ISG15**  | Down          | ISGylation, immune modulation    | Type I IFN signaling   |  \n| **MAPK1**  | Up            | Pro-inflammatory MAPK signaling  | MAPK pathway           |  \n| **HLA-A**  | Up            | Antigen presentation             | Adaptive immunity      |  \n| **CFB**    | Down          | Complement cascade activation    | Complement system      |  \n\n---\n\n### **Comparisons**  \n- **irMyositis vs. DM**: DM shows stronger upregulation of ISGs (e.g., MX1, ISG15) compared to irMyositis, suggesting distinct immune activation mechanisms.  \n- **irMyositis vs. NDC**: Upregulation of HLA and MAPK1 in irMyositis highlights antigen-driven inflammation.  \n- **DM vs. NDC**: S100A7 and CCL22 are downregulated in DM, contrasting with irMyositis vs. NDC patterns.\n\n---\n\n### **Implications**  \n- **irMyositis**: Enrichment for MAPK and HLA pathways suggests T-cell-mediated inflammation.  \n- **DM**: Strong IFN signaling aligns with classical autoimmune pathology.  \n- **Therapeutic relevance**: Targeting IFN signaling (DM) vs. MAPK/HLA pathways (irMyositis) may require distinct approaches.\n\n---\n\n### **Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. **Provide GSEA results**: Upload Reactome pathway annotations or ranked gene lists for GSEA analysis.  \n2. **Validate interferon signaling**: Confirm type I IFN pathway activity in DM vs. irMyositis using qPCR or protein data.  \n3. **Complement system role**: Investigate CFB downregulation in irMyositis for potential regulatory mechanisms.  \n4. **HLA-CXCR6 axis**: Explore HLA upregulation and CXCR6 induction in irMyositis for T-cell infiltration markers.\n\n---\n\n### **References**  \nNot applicable (no PMIDs validated in this analysis).  \n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**: The DGE data highlights divergent immune pathways between irMyositis and DM, but pathway-level GSEA requires additional inputs. Focus on IFN signaling (DM) and MAPK/HLA pathways (irMyositis) for mechanistic insights.",
